        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(session-memory): bound session transcript reads to prevent OOM",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\n`getRecentSessionContent` in `src/hooks/bundled/session-memory/transcript.ts` reads session transcript JSONL files with an unbounded `fs.promises.readFile` call. A runaway or corrupted session file could force the session-memory extraction path to buffer the entire file into memory, leading to OOM.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nSession transcripts are line-oriented JSONL files. A 16 MiB cap is generous headroom for long sessions while preventing the memory-extraction path from being used as a memory exhaustion vector. When the cap is exceeded the function returns `null`, which is the existing failure fallback.\n\n## User Impact\n\nSession-memory extraction now safely skips oversized transcripts instead of risking process OOM.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Added a regression test that writes a transcript exceeding the 16 MiB cap and asserts `getRecentSessionContent` returns `null`.\n- Local focused run: `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/hooks/bundled/session-memory/transcript.test.ts --run` passes (4/4 tests).\n\n\n## Real behavior proof\n\nI created a session transcript larger than the new 16 MiB cap and ran `getRecentSessionContent`. The helper returns `null` instead of buffering the oversized file:\n\n```text\n$ OPENCLAW_VITEST_MAX_WORKERS=1 node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/hooks/bundled/session-memory/transcript.test.ts --run\n[test] starting test/vitest/vitest.hooks.config.ts\n RUN  v4.1.9\n ✓ |hooks| src/hooks/bundled/session-memory/transcript.test.ts (4 tests) 329ms\n     ✓ returns null for oversized transcripts instead of buffering them\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  4 passed (4)\n[test] passed 1 Vitest shard in 12.39s\n```\n\nThe oversized transcript path is rejected with `File exceeds 16777216 bytes`, and the memory-extraction path receives an empty result rather than attempting to parse the huge file.",
        "branch": "fix/bound-session-memory-transcript-read",
